==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}
From {{der|en|fr|festif}}, from {{der|en|la|festivus|t=pertaining to a feast, gay, lively, joyous}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|feast|-ive}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɛstɪv/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-festive.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|fes|tive}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛstɪv|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having the [[atmosphere]], [[decoration]], or [[attitude]] of a [[festival]], [[holiday]], or [[celebration]].
#: {{ux|en|The room was decked out in '''festive''' streamers, with flowers everywhere.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Siegfried Sassoon|title=The Old Century and seven more years
|location=London|publisher=Faber|year=1938|page=35 (1968 edition)
|passage=On '''festive''' occasions away from home we softened under the influence of Christmas trees, bran pies, and conjurors.}}
# In the mood to [[celebrate]].
#: {{ux|en|Please put the Christmas decorations away, I'm really not in a '''festive''' mood.}}

